answer:
Don’t build a reusable form framework. Build a **small form boundary system**: shared plumbing, local forms.

The goal is not “all forms are configurable.”  
The goal is “all forms have the same seams.”

## Architecture: Form Recipes + Thin Plumbing

Each form owns its fields and layout, but follows a small contract:

```ts
type FormRecipe<TInput, TResult> = {
  defaults: TInput;
  validate: (input: TInput) => ValidationErrors<TInput>;
  submit: (input: TInput) => Promise<TResult>;
};
```

Then each actual form is still a normal React component.

```tsx
function ProfileForm() {
  const form = useFormRecipe(profileFormRecipe);

  return (
    <form onSubmit={form.handleSubmit}>
      <TextField
        label="Display name"
        value={form.values.displayName}
        error={form.errors.displayName}
        onChange={form.bind("displayName")}
      />

      <EmailField
        label="Email"
        value={form.values.email}
        error={form.errors.email}
        onChange={form.bind("email")}
      />

      <Button disabled={form.submitting}>Save</Button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

The shared part is tiny:

```ts
function useFormRecipe<TInput, TResult>(
  recipe: FormRecipe<TInput, TResult>
) {
  const [values, setValues] = useState(recipe.defaults);
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState({});
  const [submitting, setSubmitting] = useState(false);

  function bind<K extends keyof TInput>(key: K) {
    return (value: TInput[K]) => {
      setValues((prev) => ({ ...prev, [key]: value }));
    };
  }

  async function handleSubmit(event: React.FormEvent) {
    event.preventDefault();

    const nextErrors = recipe.validate(values);
    setErrors(nextErrors);

    if (Object.keys(nextErrors).length > 0) return;

    setSubmitting(true);
    try {
      await recipe.submit(values);
    } finally {
      setSubmitting(false);
    }
  }

  return {
    values,
    errors,
    submitting,
    bind,
    handleSubmit,
  };
}
```

## Clear boundaries

### 1. Form component owns layout

The form component decides:

- field order
- conditional sections
- copy
- grouping
- buttons
- help text
- responsive behavior

Example:

```tsx
<ProfileForm />
<BillingAddressForm />
<TeamInviteForm />
```

These should not be generated from one massive config object.

---

### 2. Recipe owns business behavior

Each form gets a nearby recipe:

```ts
// profileForm.recipe.ts

export const profileFormRecipe = {
  defaults: {
    displayName: "",
    email: "",
  },

  validate(values) {
    const errors = {};

    if (!values.displayName) {
      errors.displayName = "Display name is required";
    }

    if (!values.email.includes("@")) {
      errors.email = "Enter a valid email";
    }

    return errors;
  },

  async submit(values) {
    return updateProfile(values);
  },
};
```

This keeps the logic reusable without making the UI generic.

---

### 3. Field components own input mechanics

Shared field components should be boring and narrow:

```tsx
<TextField />
<SelectField />
<CheckboxField />
<DateField />
<MoneyField />
```

They own:

- label rendering
- error rendering
- accessibility IDs
- formatting/parsing if needed
- consistent styling

They should not know about the whole form.

```tsx
function TextField({ label, value, error, onChange }) {
  return (
    <label>
      <span>{label}</span>
      <input value={value} onChange={(e) => onChange(e.target.value)} />
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
    </label>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Validation lives beside the recipe

Use whatever you already use: hand-written validation, Zod, Yup, Valibot, etc.

The key rule:

> Validation is shared through schemas/functions, not through a giant form renderer.

Example with Zod:

```ts
const profileSchema = z.object({
  displayName: z.string().min(1, "Display name is required"),
  email: z.string().email("Enter a valid email"),
});

export const profileFormRecipe = {
  defaults: {
    displayName: "",
    email: "",
  },

  validate(values) {
    const result = profileSchema.safeParse(values);

    if (result.success) return {};

    return zodErrorsToFormErrors(result.error);
  },

  submit(values) {
    return updateProfile(values);
  },
};
```

---

## Suggested folder shape

```txt
src/
  forms/
    useFormRecipe.ts
    validation.ts
    fieldTypes.ts

  components/
    fields/
      TextField.tsx
      EmailField.tsx
      SelectField.tsx
      CheckboxField.tsx
      DateField.tsx

  features/
    profile/
      ProfileForm.tsx
      profileForm.recipe.ts
      profileForm.schema.ts

    billing/
      BillingAddressForm.tsx
      billingAddressForm.recipe.ts
      billingAddressForm.schema.ts

    teams/
      TeamInviteForm.tsx
      teamInviteForm.recipe.ts
      teamInviteForm.schema.ts
```

The shared `forms/` folder stays small.  
Most form knowledge stays inside the feature.

## What not to abstract

Avoid abstracting these too early:

```tsx
<UniversalForm config={profileConfig} />
```

or:

```ts
const profileFormConfig = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Profile",
      fields: [
        {
          type: "text",
          name: "displayName",
          label: "Display name",
          validators: ["required"],
          layout: { colSpan: 6 },
          permissions: ["admin"],
          visibleWhen: "...",
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
```

That looks reusable, but it usually becomes a private language for building forms. Then every real product requirement becomes “add another option to the config.”

## Better abstraction rule

Only extract the part that has repeated for at least three forms.

Good abstractions:

```tsx
<TextField />
<ServerErrorBanner />
<SubmitButton />
useFormRecipe()
zodErrorsToFormErrors()
```

Risky abstractions:

```tsx
<FormBuilder />
<FieldRenderer />
<FormConfigInterpreter />
<DynamicConditionalSectionEngine />
```

## The smaller contract

Each form should answer four questions:

```ts
const recipe = {
  defaults,  // What does the form start with?
  validate,  // What is invalid?
  submit,    // What happens on success?
};
```

Each component answers one question:

```tsx
function Form() {
  // What should the user see?
}
```

That boundary is the architecture.

## Why this works better than a giant framework

A giant form framework tries to make every form the same.

This approach makes every form **structured the same way**, while letting each form remain specific.

You get:

- repeated logic removed
- validation standardized
- submission standardized
- fields visually consistent
- layout still flexible
- no custom form DSL
- no monster config object
- easy debugging

The architecture is small enough to delete later, which is usually a good sign.

Creative move: subtracted the “reusable form” idea down to reusable seams instead of reusable forms.

## Proposal: “Policy Capsules” instead of a DDD rewrite

Do not introduce a full domain layer yet. Instead, extract scattered business rules into small, pure TypeScript modules called **policies**.

A policy answers one business question:

- “Can this user cancel this order?”
- “Is this customer eligible for free shipping?”
- “What discount applies?”
- “Should this payout be held for review?”

Routes can keep doing orchestration for now, but they stop owning business decisions.

---

## Target shape

```txt
src/
  routes/
    orders.routes.ts

  policies/
    orderCancellation.policy.ts
    freeShipping.policy.ts
    payoutHold.policy.ts

  application/
    cancelOrder.action.ts        // optional later step

  services/
    orderService.ts
    paymentService.ts
```

The key idea:

```ts
// routes can gather facts
// policies make decisions
// services perform effects
```

---

## A small policy format

Create a shared decision type:

```ts
// src/policies/Decision.ts

export type Decision<T = void> =
  | {
      allowed: true;
      value: T;
      reasons?: string[];
    }
  | {
      allowed: false;
      code: string;
      message: string;
      reasons?: string[];
    };
```

Then write policies as pure functions:

```ts
// src/policies/orderCancellation.policy.ts

import { Decision } from "./Decision";

type OrderCancellationFacts = {
  orderStatus: "pending" | "paid" | "shipped" | "delivered" | "cancelled";
  placedAt: Date;
  now: Date;
  userId: string;
  orderUserId: string;
  hasOpenChargeback: boolean;
};

export function canCancelOrder(
  facts: OrderCancellationFacts
): Decision {
  if (facts.userId !== facts.orderUserId) {
    return {
      allowed: false,
      code: "NOT_ORDER_OWNER",
      message: "Only the owner of the order can cancel it.",
    };
  }

  if (facts.hasOpenChargeback) {
    return {
      allowed: false,
      code: "OPEN_CHARGEBACK",
      message: "Orders with an open chargeback cannot be cancelled.",
    };
  }

  if (facts.orderStatus === "shipped" || facts.orderStatus === "delivered") {
    return {
      allowed: false,
      code: "ORDER_ALREADY_FULFILLED",
      message: "Shipped or delivered orders cannot be cancelled.",
    };
  }

  const hoursSincePlaced =
    (facts.now.getTime() - facts.placedAt.getTime()) / 1000 / 60 / 60;

  if (hoursSincePlaced > 24) {
    return {
      allowed: false,
      code: "CANCELLATION_WINDOW_EXPIRED",
      message: "Orders can only be cancelled within 24 hours.",
    };
  }

  return {
    allowed: true,
    value: undefined,
    reasons: ["Order is cancellable."],
  };
}
```

The route still looks familiar:

```ts
// src/routes/orders.routes.ts

import { canCancelOrder } from "../policies/orderCancellation.policy";

router.post("/:orderId/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const order = await orderService.getById(req.params.orderId);
  const user = req.user;

  if (!order) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: "Order not found" });
  }

  const hasOpenChargeback = await paymentService.hasOpenChargeback(order.id);

  const decision = canCancelOrder({
    orderStatus: order.status,
    placedAt: order.placedAt,
    now: new Date(),
    userId: user.id,
    orderUserId: order.userId,
    hasOpenChargeback,
  });

  if (!decision.allowed) {
    return res.status(400).json({
      code: decision.code,
      message: decision.message,
    });
  }

  await orderService.cancel(order.id);

  return res.status(204).send();
});
```

This is not “pure architecture” yet, but it creates a seam.

---

## Why this is smaller than DDD

You are not introducing:

- aggregates
- repositories
- entities
- value objects everywhere
- bounded contexts
- domain events
- command buses
- a full use-case layer
- a new folder hierarchy for the entire app

You are only introducing:

1. pure functions for business rules
2. typed inputs called facts
3. typed outputs called decisions
4. tests around those decisions

That is enough to start draining business logic out of route handlers.

---

## The adoption rule

Use this rule:

> Whenever a route contains a business `if`, extract that condition into a policy.

Not every `if` is business logic.

These can stay in routes:

```ts
if (!req.body.email) ...
if (!order) ...
if (!req.user) ...
```

These should move to policies:

```ts
if (order.status === "shipped") ...
if (customer.lifetimeSpend > 500) ...
if (payout.amount > user.approvedLimit) ...
if (subscription.plan === "trial" && feature.requiresPaidPlan) ...
```

Routes can validate transport concerns. Policies own product decisions.

---

## Add tests where they matter most

Policy tests are cheap because policies are pure.

```ts
import { canCancelOrder } from "./orderCancellation.policy";

describe("canCancelOrder", () => {
  it("rejects shipped orders", () => {
    const decision = canCancelOrder({
      orderStatus: "shipped",
      placedAt: new Date("2025-01-01T10:00:00Z"),
      now: new Date("2025-01-01T11:00:00Z"),
      userId: "user_1",
      orderUserId: "user_1",
      hasOpenChargeback: false,
    });

    expect(decision).toMatchObject({
      allowed: false,
      code: "ORDER_ALREADY_FULFILLED",
    });
  });

  it("allows pending orders inside the cancellation window", () => {
    const decision = canCancelOrder({
      orderStatus: "pending",
      placedAt: new Date("2025-01-01T10:00:00Z"),
      now: new Date("2025-01-01T11:00:00Z"),
      userId: "user_1",
      orderUserId: "user_1",
      hasOpenChargeback: false,
    });

    expect(decision.allowed).toBe(true);
  });
});
```

This gives you domain-level safety without domain-level ceremony.

---

## Incremental migration plan

### Step 1: Pick one painful route

Choose a route with:

- many conditionals
- frequent bugs
- unclear business behavior
- repeated rules elsewhere

Do not start globally.

---

### Step 2: Extract one policy

Before:

```ts
if (order.status === "shipped" || order.status === "delivered") {
  return res.status(400).json({
    error: "Cannot cancel fulfilled orders",
  });
}
```

After:

```ts
const decision = canCancelOrder(facts);

if (!decision.allowed) {
  return res.status(400).json({
    code: decision.code,
    message: decision.message,
  });
}
```

Keep the route otherwise unchanged.

---

### Step 3: Use “facts” as the boundary

Policies should not call databases, APIs, queues, or services.

Bad:

```ts
export async function canCancelOrder(orderId: string) {
  const order = await orderService.getById(orderId);
  // ...
}
```

Better:

```ts
export function canCancelOrder(facts: OrderCancellationFacts) {
  // pure business decision
}
```

The route gathers facts. The policy decides.

This prevents policies from becoming hidden service layers.

---

### Step 4: Remove duplication by reusing policies

If another route has similar logic:

```ts
router.delete("/:orderId", async (req, res) => {
  // same cancellation rules again
});
```

Replace it with the same policy:

```ts
const decision = canCancelOrder(facts);
```

Now the business rule has one home.

---

### Step 5: Only introduce actions when routes become noisy

After a few policies, some routes may still be too large because they gather many facts and perform many effects.

Then introduce a small application action:

```ts
// src/application/cancelOrder.action.ts

import { canCancelOrder } from "../policies/orderCancellation.policy";

export async function cancelOrder(input: {
  orderId: string;
  userId: string;
}) {
  const order = await orderService.getById(input.orderId);

  if (!order) {
    return {
      ok: false as const,
      status: 404,
      code: "ORDER_NOT_FOUND",
    };
  }

  const hasOpenChargeback = await paymentService.hasOpenChargeback(order.id);

  const decision = canCancelOrder({
    orderStatus: order.status,
    placedAt: order.placedAt,
    now: new Date(),
    userId: input.userId,
    orderUserId: order.userId,
    hasOpenChargeback,
  });

  if (!decision.allowed) {
    return {
      ok: false as const,
      status: 400,
      code: decision.code,
      message: decision.message,
    };
  }

  await orderService.cancel(order.id);

  return {
    ok: true as const,
  };
}
```

Then the route becomes thin:

```ts
router.post("/:orderId/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const result = await cancelOrder({
    orderId: req.params.orderId,
    userId: req.user.id,
  });

  if (!result.ok) {
    return res.status(result.status).json(result);
  }

  return res.status(204).send();
});
```

Important: this comes later. Do not start by creating actions for everything.

---

## Naming convention

Use question-style names for policies:

```ts
canCancelOrder()
canAccessFeature()
canRefundPayment()
shouldHoldPayout()
calculateDiscount()
selectShippingMethod()
```

This makes route code readable:

```ts
const decision = canRefundPayment(facts);
```

or:

```ts
const discount = calculateDiscount(facts);
```

Avoid vague names:

```ts
OrderDomainService
BusinessRulesManager
EligibilityProcessor
PolicyEngine
```

Those names become dumping grounds.

---

## Optional guardrail: ban business logic from routes gradually

Once the pattern has proven useful, add a lightweight lint/review rule:

> Route handlers may not contain business decisions involving domain fields.

Examples to flag in review:

```ts
if (order.status === "paid") ...
if (user.plan === "enterprise") ...
if (invoice.total > 10000) ...
```

Preferred:

```ts
const decision = canDoThing(facts);
```

You do not need to automate this immediately. A checklist in PR review may be enough.

---

## When this architecture is enough

This works well when:

- the main pain is duplicated or hidden rules
- route handlers are too smart
- the team wants better tests
- a DDD rewrite would stall
- the domain is evolving and you do not yet know the right object model

It gives you most of the immediate value:

- central rules
- better names
- simple tests
- less route complexity
- incremental migration

without forcing a full domain model too early.

---

## When to evolve beyond it

Eventually, if you see policies sharing the same clusters of facts repeatedly, that is a signal.

For example, many policies require:

```ts
user.plan
user.region
user.accountAge
user.riskScore
subscription.status
```

At that point, you may introduce richer concepts:

```ts
Account
Subscription
RiskProfile
Entitlement
```

But now those abstractions are discovered from extracted rules, not invented upfront.

---

## Summary

Adopt a **Policy Capsule** architecture:

```txt
Route gathers facts.
Policy makes a pure business decision.
Service performs side effects.
Action layer appears only when orchestration gets bulky.
```

This is small enough to adopt one route at a time, but strong enough to stop business rules from leaking everywhere.

Creative move: Instead of replacing the app with a domain model, create a “business-rule firewall” made of tiny pure decision functions that slowly drains logic out of routes.

Here’s a debugging and fix plan that treats the German translation as valid content, not a problem to edit away.

## 1. Reproduce and isolate the failure

First, identify *what kind* of breakage is happening:

- Text overflows horizontally
- Button label wraps to two lines and breaks height
- Flex/grid item refuses to shrink
- Card heights become uneven
- Text overlaps icon or neighboring column
- Modal/header/footer exceeds viewport
- German compound word does not wrap

Use DevTools with the German locale active and inspect the failing element.

Check:

```css
width
min-width
max-width
height
min-height
white-space
overflow
text-overflow
display
flex
grid-template-columns
align-items
```

Also inspect parent containers. Often the bug is not on the text node but on a parent with a fixed width, fixed height, or rigid flex behavior.

---

## 2. Treat German as a layout stress test

German exposes layout assumptions because it often has:

- Longer phrases
- Compound nouns
- Fewer natural break points
- Longer button labels
- Different sentence order
- More formal/legal wording

So the goal is not “make German shorter.”

The goal is:

> The component should survive legitimate localized text within a reasonable expansion range.

As a baseline, test with:

- German
- Finnish
- Dutch
- Polish
- pseudo-localized English, e.g.  
  `[[ Šëttïñğš añð prëfërëñçës cõñfïgüråtïõñ ]]`

Pseudo-localization is especially useful because it catches the same class of bugs before translators are involved.

---

## 3. Find the rigid constraint

Look for these common offenders.

### Fixed widths

Bad:

```css
.button {
  width: 120px;
}
```

Better:

```css
.button {
  min-inline-size: 120px;
  max-inline-size: 100%;
}
```

Or let the content define the size:

```css
.button {
  inline-size: auto;
  max-inline-size: 100%;
}
```

---

### Fixed heights

Bad:

```css
.card-title {
  height: 24px;
}
```

Better:

```css
.card-title {
  min-block-size: 24px;
}
```

If the component needs consistent visual rhythm, use spacing and alignment instead of forcing single-line text.

---

### Flex children that cannot shrink

This is one of the most common localization bugs.

Bad:

```css
.row {
  display: flex;
}

.label {
  flex: 1;
}
```

A flex child’s default `min-width: auto` can prevent shrinking.

Fix:

```css
.row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 0.5rem;
}

.label {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-inline-size: 0;
}
```

If text should wrap:

```css
.label {
  min-inline-size: 0;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
```

If text should truncate:

```css
.label {
  min-inline-size: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
```

Use truncation carefully. It may be okay for secondary metadata, but not for primary actions, legal text, error messages, or form labels.

---

### Grid columns that refuse to shrink

Bad:

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
}
```

Better:

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
}
```

For multiple fluid columns:

```css
.grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(16rem, 100%), 1fr));
}
```

---

## 4. Add language-aware wrapping

German compound words may need explicit wrapping support.

Set the document language correctly:

```html
<html lang="de">
```

Or on a localized section:

```html
<div lang="de">
  ...
</div>
```

Then enable hyphenation:

```css
.localized-text {
  hyphens: auto;
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
}
```

For especially constrained UI, use:

```css
.localized-text {
  hyphens: auto;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
```

Prefer this order:

1. Normal wrapping
2. `hyphens: auto`
3. `overflow-wrap: break-word`
4. `overflow-wrap: anywhere` only when layout survival matters more than pretty word shape

Avoid using `word-break: break-all` for German body text. It produces ugly, hard-to-read breaks.

---

## 5. Decide per component: wrap, grow, truncate, or reflow

Not every piece of text should behave the same way.

### Primary buttons

Prefer allowing wrapping or expanding:

```css
.button {
  max-inline-size: 100%;
  white-space: normal;
  text-align: center;
  line-height: 1.2;
  padding-block: 0.625rem;
  padding-inline: 1rem;
}
```

For button groups, allow wrapping:

```css
.button-row {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.75rem;
}
```

Do not force three German buttons into one tiny row just because English fits.

---

### Navigation tabs

For tabs, consider horizontal scrolling or responsive collapse:

```css
.tabs {
  display: flex;
  overflow-x: auto;
  scrollbar-width: thin;
}

.tab {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}
```

For narrow containers, switch to a dropdown or stacked layout.

---

### Cards

Avoid assuming title length:

```css
.card {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}

.card-title {
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
  hyphens: auto;
}

.card-actions {
  margin-block-start: auto;
}
```

If card heights must align, let the whole card stretch rather than clipping localized text.

---

### Forms

Labels should be allowed to wrap:

```css
.form-row {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(8rem, 14rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 0.75rem;
}

.form-label {
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
  hyphens: auto;
}
```

On small screens:

```css
@media (max-width: 40rem) {
  .form-row {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
```

---

## 6. Remove English-shaped assumptions

Audit the component for assumptions like:

```css
white-space: nowrap;
height: 40px;
line-height: 40px;
width: 180px;
position: absolute;
left: 120px;
```

Replace with logical, flexible sizing:

```css
inline-size
block-size
min-inline-size
max-inline-size
padding-inline
padding-block
margin-inline
```

This also helps with RTL locales later.

Example:

```css
.badge {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  min-block-size: 2rem;
  padding-inline: 0.75rem;
  padding-block: 0.25rem;
  max-inline-size: 100%;
}
```

---

## 7. Add a localization layout contract

Instead of asking translators to shorten text, define how each component behaves with expansion.

Example contract:

| Component | Behavior with long localized text |
|---|---|
| Primary button | May wrap to 2 lines |
| Secondary metadata | May truncate with tooltip/accessibility label |
| Form label | Wraps naturally |
| Navigation item | Scrolls or collapses on small widths |
| Card title | Wraps up to 2–3 lines, then layout expands or truncates depending on priority |
| Error message | Never truncates |

This makes localization part of the design system instead of an afterthought.

---

## 8. Add regression tests

Create visual tests for:

- German locale
- longest known translation strings
- pseudo-localized strings
- narrow container widths
- mobile viewport
- increased browser font size
- 200% zoom

If you use Storybook, add stories like:

```js
export const GermanLongText = {
  args: {
    label: 'Datenschutzeinstellungen konfigurieren',
  },
};

